course: dictionary
lesson: 16112
title: The state of being wet
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Wetness is a noun. It names the state of being wet, when something is covered in water or another liquid, or is full of it. You can feel wetness on your hands or see it on a soggy towel.
explain: Look at the word parts: wet + ness. The ending -ness turns a describing word into a naming word, as kind becomes kindness and sad becomes sadness. Wet tells you what something is like, and wetness is the name for that state.
explain: «The wetness of the sponge showed how much water it could hold.» Here wetness means how wet the sponge is, not the sponge itself. You cannot count wetness, so you say some wetness, not three wetnesses.
fact: Wetness is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say «some wetness», not «a wetness».
fact: Wetness means the state of being wet, covered in or full of water or another liquid.
fact: The ending -ness changes a describing word into a naming word: wet becomes wetness, dry becomes dryness.
fact: «The wetness soaked through his coat before he reached the bus stop.»
exercise: «The wetness soaked through his coat.» What does wetness mean here? | A small hole in the cloth | *The state of being covered in water | A kind of warm, sunny weather | The feeling of being very tired
exercise: Which ending turns wet into the naming word wetness? | *-ness | -y | -est | -ly
exercise: «The scientists measured the wetness of each cloth.» What were they measuring? | *How much water each cloth held | How old each cloth was | How many threads each cloth had | How loud each cloth was
test: Which is correct? | two wetnesses | three wetnesses | *some wetness | a wetness
test: What part of speech is wetness? | adverb | adjective | *noun | verb
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to wetness? | coldness | brightness | hardness | *dampness
